From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWrt <noreply@openwrt.org>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: flush airoha PPE on generic STA removal

Commit c1f6d6ba6f ("airoha: fix WiFi roaming PPE stall") added 0013,
which calls airoha_ppe_dev_flush_flows_by_mac() from the mt7996-specific
per-link path mt7996_mac_sta_remove_link().  However that path only runs
for mt7996 and only for per-link MLO teardown; the generic mt76
__mt76_sta_remove() callback that drives sta_remove for *all* mt76
chips does not feed the PPE L2/L4 caches on STA teardown.

When a STA is torn down due to an explicit disconnect (rather than a
bridge-side FDB port move), mac80211 emits SWITCHDEV_FDB_DEL_TO_DEVICE
from __sta_info_destroy_part2() (commit 8a6e9740be) which the airoha
PPE switchdev notifier receives asynchronously and drains the L2 cache
via airoha_ppe_schedule_flush_by_mac().  Reaching the same drain
through the mt76 STA-removal path as well reduces the window during
which a new STA reusing the same MAC could collide with stale PPE L2
entries, and extends the same coverage to any mt76 chip that does not
implement a driver-specific sta_remove_link hook.

Add a small inline block in __mt76_sta_remove() that mirrors the
mt7996_ppe_flush_sta_addr() helper introduced by 0013 (rcu-protected
dev->mmio.ppe_dev lookup + airoha_ppe_dev_flush_flows_by_mac() inline
wrapper defined in <linux/soc/airoha/airoha_offload.h> by 930) and
invoke it immediately after drv->sta_remove returns and before
mt76_wcid_cleanup() drops the WCID, so the PPE L2 flush is queued
before the WCID/card state referenced by the flow entries is released.

No new kernel-tree symbols are introduced: this relies entirely on the
inline wrapper added to <linux/soc/airoha/airoha_offload.h> by patch
930.  Guard the include with CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_NPU the same way mt76.h
already guards the airoha_offload.h selection, so non-airoha targets
compile unchanged.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <noreply@openwrt.org>
---
--- a/mac80211.c
+++ b/mac80211.c
@@ -4,6 +4,9 @@
  */
 #include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_NPU)
+#include <linux/soc/airoha/airoha_offload.h>
+#endif
 #include "mt76.h"
 
 static void
@@ -1630,6 +1633,28 @@ void __mt76_sta_remove(struct mt76_phy *phy, struct ieee80211_vif *vif,
 	if (dev->drv->sta_remove)
 		dev->drv->sta_remove(dev, vif, sta);
 
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_NPU)
+	/* Drain any PPE L2/L4 entries bound to this STA's MAC before the
+	 * WCID is freed.  Same-BSS disconnect/reconnect often does not
+	 * move the bridge FDB port, so the airoha PPE switchdev notifier
+	 * (which catches SWITCHDEV_FDB_DEL_TO_DEVICE from mac80211) may
+	 * either not fire at all or run asynchronously after the WCID is
+	 * gone.  Calling airoha_ppe_dev_flush_flows_by_mac() here queues
+	 * the L2 flush via eth->fdb_wq on the airoha side; the WCID we
+	 * hold here is not referenced by the queued work (it keys only
+	 * off sta->addr), so dropping wcid below is safe.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct airoha_ppe_dev *ppe_dev;
+
+		rcu_read_lock();
+		ppe_dev = rcu_dereference(dev->mmio.ppe_dev);
+		if (ppe_dev)
+			airoha_ppe_dev_flush_flows_by_mac(ppe_dev, sta->addr);
+		rcu_read_unlock();
+	}
+#endif
+
 	mt76_wcid_cleanup(dev, wcid);
 
 	mt76_wcid_mask_clear(dev->wcid_mask, idx);
